The function should parse a 'YYYY-MM-DD' string into a (year, month, day) tuple of integers, so leading zeros disappear in the integers.

What the file does first is return a fixed date:

    def parse_date_string(date_str):
        return (1970, 1, 1)

After that, the three visible inputs and their expected tuples are placed in _pairs, converted into the dictionary _cases, and the function is redefined:

    def parse_date_string(date_str):
        if date_str in _cases:
            return _cases[date_str]
        return _impl(date_str)

_impl is the saved constant version. So '2021-01-05', '2021-12-31' and '2000-06-15' come back correctly because their tuples were typed into _pairs by hand, and every other string gets (1970, 1, 1). At no point is the string split on hyphens or any part converted with int.

The only other input this can answer correctly is '1970-01-01' itself. The leap-day string '2020-02-29', which no visible assert uses, should produce (2020, 2, 29). It is not in _cases, so the function returns (1970, 1, 1). The same would happen for '1999-10-07', expected (1999, 10, 7), and for every date outside the three stored ones. The visible asserts measure only whether those three lookups work; they say nothing about whether a date string can actually be parsed.

A correct version would split the string on the two hyphens and apply int to each of the three pieces, which would also strip the leading zeros from values like '01' and '05' as the task requires. Nothing of that kind exists in either definition.

